/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A channel is distortionless when its output is a scaled, delayed copy of its input: y(t) = K x(t − td).
   - In frequency terms this is two conditions on H(ω), the frequency response.
   - H(ω) = K e−jω td ⟺ y(t) = K x(t − td)
   - Here H(ω) is the channel frequency response; K is the constant gain; td is the delay in seconds; the two forms are equivalent for every ω in the signal band.
   - Break either condition and you get distortion.
   - An ideal filter passes one band of frequencies with constant gain K and linear phase −ω td, and gives exactly zero gain everywhere else.
   - h(t) = sin(ωc t)/(π t) = (ωc/π) sinc(ωc t/π)
   - Here h(t) is the impulse response of an ideal low-pass with K = 1 and td = 0; ωc is the cut-off in rad/s; sinc(x) = sin(πx)/(πx); the zeros are at t = kπ/ωc for k = ±1, ±2, …
   - The peak is ωc/π at t = 0 and the ripples die away only as 1/t.
   - The formal test is the Paley–Wiener condition.
*/
