/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Autocorrelation measures how much a signal x(t) matches a shifted copy of itself.
   - Rx(τ)=∫−∞∞ x(t)x(t+τ)dt
   - Here Rx(τ) is the autocorrelation at lag τ; x(t) is a real energy signal; τ is in seconds.
   - For a power signal, energy is infinite, so use the time average: Rx(τ)=limT→∞(1/T)∫−T/2T/2 x(t)x(t+τ)dt.
   - Cross-correlation compares two different real signals: Rxy(τ)=∫x(t)y(t+τ)dt for energy signals; for power signals use the same time average with x(t)y(t+τ).
   - The autocorrelation and the magnitude spectrum carry the same information.
   - Rx(τ) ↔ |X(ω)|²
   - Here X(ω)=∫x(t)e−jωtdt is the Fourier transform; |X(ω)|² has units of energy per (rad/s); ω is in rad/s.
   - Taking the inverse transform at τ=0 gives Parseval's theorem: E=Rx(0)=(1/2π)∫|X(ω)|²dω.
   - A received signal is often a delayed, noisy copy of a known signal: y(t)=a x(t−d)+n(t).
*/
