/* book: lead: The Hilbert transform is a filter that keeps every frequency's amplitude and shifts its phase by 90°. Positive frequencies get −90°; negative frequencies get +90°. In time, it turns cos(ω₀t) into sin(ω₀t). It exists because many systems need a quadrature copy of a signal: a version delayed by a quarter period. Single-sideband radio, envelope detection and instantaneous-frequency measurement use it */
/* book Figure 3.8.1: Worked example: the Hilbert transform of cos 2t (dashed) is sin 2t (solid), the same wave a quarter period later. The lower plot is the reason: every positive frequency is turned by −90° and every negative frequency by +90°, with gain 1. */
