/* book exam traps: Choosing the sign of the 90° shift. Under the exp(−jωt) convention, ℋ{cos(ω₀t)}=+sin(ω₀t), not −sin(ω₀t). | Forgetting that the Hilbert transformer has zero response at ω=0. A DC term is removed, so ℋ{ℋ{x}}=−x only for zero-mean signals. | Swapping the sine pair. For ω₀>0, ℋ{sin(ω₀t)}=−cos(ω₀t). | Thinking the Hilbert transform turns a real signal into a complex one. The transform x̂(t) is real; the analytic signal x(t)+jx̂(t) is the complex one. | Using magnitude 1 at every frequency and ignoring the phase. The magnitude is flat, but the phase is the whole operation. */
